{"subjectId":"sql","id":"gaps-and-islands","title":"Consecutive Days, Gaps & Islands","group":"Interview Prep","order":59,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">“Users who logged in 3 days in a row”, “longest streak”, “missing ids” — the <strong>gaps-and-islands</strong> family, solved elegantly with <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> or <code>LAG</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT user_id, group_concat(substr(login_date, 9), ', ') AS march_days\nFROM (SELECT * FROM logins ORDER BY user_id, login_date)\nGROUP BY user_id\nORDER BY user_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>user_id | march_days\n--------+-------------------\n1       | 01, 02, 03, 05, 06\n2       | 01, 03\n3       | 02, 03, 04, 05</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bulb\"></i>The islands trick</h3>\n<p>Within one user, subtract the row number (in days) from the date. Consecutive dates produce the <strong>same</strong> anchor value; a gap changes it. Group by that anchor and each group is one streak (“island”).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT user_id, login_date,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) AS rn,\n       date(login_date, '-' || ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) || ' days') AS grp\nFROM logins\nWHERE user_id = 1\nORDER BY login_date;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>user_id | login_date | rn | grp\n--------+------------+----+-----------\n1       | 2024-03-01 | 1  | 2024-02-29\n1       | 2024-03-02 | 2  | 2024-02-29\n1       | 2024-03-03 | 3  | 2024-02-29\n1       | 2024-03-05 | 4  | 2024-03-01\n1       | 2024-03-06 | 5  | 2024-03-01</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH marked AS (\n    SELECT user_id, login_date,\n           date(login_date, '-' || ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) || ' days') AS grp\n    FROM logins\n)\nSELECT user_id, MIN(login_date) AS streak_start, MAX(login_date) AS streak_end, COUNT(*) AS days\nFROM marked\nGROUP BY user_id, grp\nORDER BY user_id, streak_start;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>user_id | streak_start | streak_end | days\n--------+--------------+------------+-----\n1       | 2024-03-01   | 2024-03-03 | 3\n1       | 2024-03-05   | 2024-03-06 | 2\n2       | 2024-03-01   | 2024-03-01 | 1\n2       | 2024-03-03   | 2024-03-03 | 1\n3       | 2024-03-02   | 2024-03-05 | 4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flame\"></i>Users with 3+ consecutive days, and longest streak</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH marked AS (\n    SELECT user_id,\n           date(login_date, '-' || ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) || ' days') AS grp\n    FROM logins\n),\nstreaks AS (\n    SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS days FROM marked GROUP BY user_id, grp\n)\nSELECT user_id, MAX(days) AS longest_streak\nFROM streaks\nGROUP BY user_id\nHAVING MAX(days) &gt;= 3\nORDER BY user_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>user_id | longest_streak\n--------+---------------\n1       | 3\n3       | 4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i>The LAG alternative: detect breaks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- 3 consecutive days = today, yesterday and the day before are all present\nSELECT DISTINCT user_id\nFROM (\n    SELECT user_id, login_date,\n           LAG(login_date, 2) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date) AS two_back\n    FROM logins\n)\nWHERE julianday(login_date) - julianday(two_back) = 2\nORDER BY user_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>user_id\n-------\n1\n3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-separator\"></i>Gaps: missing values in a sequence</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- gaps in order ids (orders 101..108 exist; pretend 103 and 104 were deleted)\nDELETE FROM order_items WHERE order_id IN (103, 104);\nDELETE FROM orders WHERE order_id IN (103, 104);\n\nSELECT order_id + 1 AS gap_start, next_id - 1 AS gap_end\nFROM (SELECT order_id, LEAD(order_id) OVER (ORDER BY order_id) AS next_id FROM orders)\nWHERE next_id - order_id &gt; 1;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>gap_start | gap_end\n----------+--------\n103       | 104</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Find users who logged in on 3 consecutive days.</summary><div><p>Either <code>LAG(login_date, 2) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date)</code> and keep rows where the date difference is exactly 2 days (deduplicate dates first), or the islands trick: <code>login_date − ROW_NUMBER()</code> days is constant across a streak, so <code>GROUP BY user_id, that_value HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= 3</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}